# CourseMates UI + Multiplayer Improvement Brief

## Objective
Improve the existing CourseMates chat, games, and media experience while preserving current product behavior unless a change is explicitly requested below.

## Priority Order
1. Functional multiplayer behavior
   - UNO game state
   - Tic-Tac-Toe game state
   - Game invitations
   - Music synchronization
2. Interaction polish
   - responsiveness
   - animations
   - visual clarity
   - mobile/desktop consistency
3. Final UI refinement
   - stronger hierarchy
   - cleaner dark-mode layout
   - less clutter and more intentional design

---

## 1) Improve the UNO Game UI
Redesign the UNO interface for both desktop and mobile so it feels like a real digital UNO table for two players.

### Requirements
- Local player's cards stay clearly visible at the bottom.
- Opponent is positioned clearly across from the local player.
- Center of the board clearly shows the draw pile and discard pile.
- Player names, card counts, turn status, and key game information are easy to understand.
- Layout must be responsive for desktop and mobile.
- Add a polished turn indicator (glow, pulse, highlight, or other subtle visual cue) when it is a player's turn.
- Card draw, card play, turn transitions, UNO calls, and victory interactions should feel smooth.
- Goal: remove the unfinished stacked layout and make the table feel like an actual multiplayer card game.

---

## 2) Fix UNO Match Completion
UNO should behave as a proper 1v1 game between the two current chat participants.

### Requirements
- Match ends only when one player successfully empties their hand.
- When a player wins:
  - clearly display the winner
  - show a proper victory state or animation
  - disable further card interaction after the match ends
  - allow both players to request or start a rematch
- Both users must receive the same final game result.

---

## 3) Move Games Inside the Chat Interface
UNO and Tic-Tac-Toe should no longer appear as standalone major interface sections.

### Requirements
- Add a Games icon or button inside the chat controls.
- When clicked, open a small game catalog with at least:
  - UNO
  - Tic-Tac-Toe
- After selecting a game, the user can invite the person they are currently chatting with.
- The game flow should feel integrated into the conversation rather than like a separate app section.

---

## 4) Fix Tic-Tac-Toe Multiplayer
The current Tic-Tac-Toe experience appears broken or non-responsive and must be repaired for real-time 2-player play.

### Requirements
- Both users see the same board state.
- Clicking a valid square updates the board for both users.
- Turns alternate correctly.
- A user cannot play during the opponent's turn.
- A square cannot be selected twice.
- Wins are detected correctly.
- Draws are detected correctly.
- The game clearly indicates whose turn it is.
- The game displays the winner or draw result.
- Both players can request or start a rematch.
- Game state remains synchronized between both users.

---

## 5) Fix Music Synchronization
Currently, shared music playback can be inconsistent and may only play for one user.

### Requirements
- Ensure both users in the chat experience the same shared music session.
- Shared actions must synchronize for both participants, including:
  - play
  - pause
  - track change
  - current song
  - playback position
  - restart
  - skip (if supported)
- Both users should remain approximately synchronized instead of drifting into separate playback states.
- Prevent unnecessary synchronization loops where one client's update repeatedly triggers the other client.

---

## 6) Simplify UNO to Two Players Only
UNO should be limited to the two users in the active chat.

### Requirements
- Remove the 4-player UNO option.
- Remove bot-player options.
- Remove player-count selectors.
- Automatically treat the two users in the current chat as the UNO players.
- Do not show multiplayer setup options that are no longer needed.
- Keep the experience focused on quick, simple 1v1 matches inside the conversation.

---

## 7) Improve the Game Invitation UI
The current invitation flow is easy to miss and should be redesigned to stand out inside the conversation.

### Requirements
- Show a proper game invitation card inside the chat.
- It must clearly display:
  - selected game
  - sender of the invitation
  - recognizable game icon or visual
  - accept button
  - decline button
- The invitation should visually stand out from normal chat messages while still fitting the CourseMates design language.
- Once accepted, both users should automatically enter or initialize the same game session.

---

## 8) Add a Time Limit to the Ice Breaker
The Ice Breaker should only appear temporarily at the start of a new chat.

### Requirements
- Show the Ice Breaker when a conversation begins.
- Keep it available for approximately 1 minute to 1 minute 30 seconds.
- After that period, automatically hide or dismiss it.
- If users begin chatting before the timer ends, it may disappear earlier.
- The timer must not constantly reset on page re-renders or normal chat updates.
- The Ice Breaker should feel temporary, not permanent.

---

## 9) Improve the Overall UI
Refine the general interface so it feels more intentional, polished, and human-designed.

### Avoid
- excessive rounded cards
- too many floating containers
- random gradients
- oversized empty sections
- excessive glow effects
- too many decorative elements
- inconsistent spacing
- repeated card-within-card layouts
- generic dashboard styling

### Prefer
- stronger visual hierarchy
- cleaner spacing consistency
- fewer unnecessary containers
- readable typography
- cohesive dark-mode design
- obvious interactive elements without visual noise
- a recognizable CourseMates identity

---

## 10) Fix the Header Options
Review and redesign the header controls to reduce clutter and improve layout quality.

### Requirements
- Keep only the most important actions immediately visible.
- Place secondary actions inside an overflow, settings, or contextual menu.
- The chat header should prioritize:
  - user/chat information
  - important conversation actions
  - games access
  - music/shared-session controls when relevant
  - settings or additional options
- Header layout must adapt properly between desktop and mobile.

---

## Overall Requirement
These improvements must work as part of the existing CourseMates application and not as disconnected mockups.

### Must preserve
- the current chat system
- the product identity
- existing app behavior unless explicitly modified above

### Priority order for implementation
1. UNO multiplayer game state
2. Tic-Tac-Toe multiplayer game state
3. Game invitations
4. Music synchronization
5. UI polish, animation, and responsiveness

This is the primary focus. Functional multiplayer reliability comes before visual refinement.